  ]}
  articleMeta={{ datePublished: '2026-09-08' }}
>

  <section class="art-hero">
    <div class="art-orb art-orb--1"></div>
    <div class="art-orb art-orb--2"></div>
    <div class="art-grid"></div>
    <div class="art-hero__inner container">
      <div class="art-meta">
        <span class="art-tag">Stripchat</span>
        <span class="art-tag art-tag--green">Guide</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="art-title">Stripchat <span class="art-grad">Fan Club Pricing</span>: Tiers That Actually Convert</h1>
      <p class="art-lead">Stripchat's Fan Club used to lock you into a handful of preset prices per tier. That's gone — you can now set a custom price for Soldier, Lord, and Prince individually, and offer 3, 6, or 12-month terms alongside the monthly option. That's a pricing decision most models are still making by guesswork. Here's how to set tiers that match what you actually offer, and how a studio should standardize the structure across a roster instead of leaving every model to price blind.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="art-body container">
    <div class="art-layout">
      <aside class="art-toc">
        <p class="art-toc__title">Contents</p>
        <ol class="art-toc__list">
          <li><a href="#sec-1">What Changed: Custom Pricing Per Tier</a></li>
          <li><a href="#sec-2">Soldier, Lord, Prince — Pricing Each Tier</a></li>
          <li><a href="#sec-3">Monthly vs. 3/6/12-Month Terms</a></li>
          <li><a href="#sec-4">Perks That Justify the Price</a></li>
          <li><a href="#sec-5">For Studios</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
        </ol>
        <a href="#cta" class="art-toc__cta">Get help →</a>
      </aside>

      <main class="art-main">

      <p class="art-intro">A recurring subscription line is the closest thing to predictable income webcam work has. Stripchat's Fan Club was already that — the recent change is that the price of each tier is now yours to set, not picked from a short list Stripchat wrote for you. A price you chose deliberately converts differently than one you copied from another model's profile.</p>
      <p class="card-date" style="margin:0 0 1.75rem;">EWOhub Editorial Team · Published Sep 8, 2026</p>

      <section class="section" id="sec-1">
        <h2>What Changed: Custom Pricing Per Tier</h2>
        <div class="glass-card" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
          <p>Fan Club has run on three tiers for a while — Soldier, Lord, and Prince, in rising order of price and perks. What's new is that you can now enter a custom amount for each tier instead of choosing from a fixed set of preset values. You're no longer boxed into whatever price bracket Stripchat decided fit that tier; you set the number based on what you're actually including at that level.</p>
          <p style="margin-bottom:0;">You can also revise prices later as your following grows — a Fan Club priced for a small, early audience doesn't have to stay that way once your traffic and reputation change. The setting lives in the same place the tiers themselves are managed, so there's no separate approval step slowing down a price update.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="sec-2">
        <h2>Soldier, Lord, Prince — Pricing Each Tier</h2>
        <div class="glass-card" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
          <p>Treat the three tiers as three different offers, not the same subscription with a bigger number attached. Soldier is the entry tier — price it low enough that a fan who likes you but hasn't committed yet doesn't have to think hard about it. Lord sits in the middle and should carry a visible perk jump, not just a price jump — this is the tier most fans should land on if the ladder is priced right. Prince is the top tier for the small number of fans who want the most access and are price-insensitive about it; underpricing Prince mostly just cannibalizes Lord.</p>
          <p style="margin-bottom:0;">A common mistake is spacing the three prices too evenly. If Soldier, Lord, and Prince sit at roughly equal intervals, there's no real incentive to skip a tier — most fans default to the cheapest option. Widen the gap between Lord and Prince specifically, since that's the upsell that funds the highest-value perks.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="sec-3">
        <h2>Monthly vs. 3/6/12-Month Terms</h2>
        <div class="glass-card" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
          <p>Alongside the standard monthly subscription, fans can now commit to 3, 6, or 12-month terms. A longer term is worth a real discount over the monthly rate — that's the entire pitch for a fan choosing it over auto-renewing month to month. The trade-off is upfront: you get more predictable income and lower monthly churn, but a discounted long-term subscriber is worth less per month than a full-price monthly one for as long as that term runs.</p>
          <p style="margin-bottom:0;">The subscription renews automatically at the tier price unless canceled, which is exactly why the initial price has to be right — a mispriced tier doesn't just lose you a one-time sale, it locks in a bad monthly number for as long as that fan stays subscribed. Fix pricing before pushing longer terms, not after.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="sec-4">
        <h2>Perks That Justify the Price</h2>

        <div class="reason-card" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
          <div class="reason-num">01</div>
          <div class="reason-content">
            <h3>Exclusive photos and videos</h3>
            <p>Content that only Fan Club members ever see is the most direct justification for the subscription price — it has to feel genuinely separate from what a free follower gets, not the same public content with a "member" label on it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="reason-card">
          <div class="reason-num">02</div>
          <div class="reason-content">
            <h3>Private messages and free show spying</h3>
            <p>Access perks — being able to message you directly, or spy on shows for free instead of paying per show — reward the subscription with ongoing access rather than a one-time content drop, which is exactly what makes a fan renew instead of buying content once and leaving.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="reason-card">
          <div class="reason-num">03</div>
          <div class="reason-content">
            <h3>Custom perks per tier</h3>
            <p>You choose which perks apply to which tier, which means the tier ladder can be built around what you're actually willing to offer rather than a template. A tier with perks you can't sustain is worse than a smaller tier you can actually deliver on every month.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="sec-5">
        <div class="highlight-box">
          <h2>For Studios: Standardizing Fan Club Structure Across a Roster</h2>
          <p style="margin-bottom:0;">Left to individual models, Fan Club pricing ends up all over the place — some models underprice out of caution, others copy a number from someone else's profile with no relationship to their own following or content output. A studio should set a baseline pricing framework per roster tier (new model, established model, top earner) rather than a single fixed price, since a one-size price ignores the real difference in what each model can deliver. Standardizing the perk structure — what Soldier/Lord/Prince includes, at minimum, across every model on the roster — also makes it easier to market Fan Club as a roster-wide feature instead of something each model figures out alone.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="faq-section section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-chevron" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>Can I change my Fan Club tier prices after fans have already subscribed?</span>
            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none"><path d="M5 7.5l5 5 5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>Yes — you can update tier prices at any time as your following grows. A price change applies going forward; it doesn't retroactively change what an already-subscribed fan is paying until their term renews.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-chevron" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>Do I need to reach a subscriber milestone before I can start a Fan Club?</span>
            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none"><path d="M5 7.5l5 5 5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>Fan Club unlocks at a subscriber milestone rather than being open from day one, so building a following on your profile and shows comes first. Use the time before you unlock it to plan the tier structure and perks, so you're not designing the ladder from scratch the day it becomes available.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-chevron" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>Should I offer all three subscription lengths — 3, 6, and 12 months — or just monthly?</span>
            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none"><path d="M5 7.5l5 5 5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>Offering the longer terms costs nothing to enable and gives price-sensitive, high-intent fans a way to lock in a discount — there's little reason to leave them off. Just make sure the discount curve is intentional: each longer term should be meaningfully cheaper per month than the one below it, or nobody has a reason to commit further out.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>Trending right now</h2>
